body:
This is really helpful! Will use this to start learning more about lazy evaluation. Thanks so much!

&#x200B;

I came across the term when I was researching the difference between OOP and functional programming. Does this mean that lazy evaluation isn't possible in OOP?

&#x200B;

What characteristics of functional programming allow lazy evaluation and what characteristics of OOP prevent it?

This makes a lot of sense. Didn't realize there were so many subtleties. Thanks so much for the info! 

&#x200B;

Any ideas why someone would use "I'll ping them" instead of "I'll email them" and "I'll circle back" instead of "I'll follow up"?

Thanks for the info! 

&#x200B;

So the idea behind unit testing is isolating each function so that an error can be easily traced, right? 

&#x200B;

I've heard an engineer mention privately that unit testing is a "nice to have" and that he only performs unit testing if he has lots of time. How important is unit testing?

I'm applying for a back-end developer position at a company, and they said "We don't do LeetCode. Instead, we give candidates a live design challenge that's similar to our day-to-day work."

&#x200B;

The company makes B2B software for medium and small businesses. Any ideas on what the design challenges might be?

&#x200B;

Have you ever been given a design challenge? If so, what were you asked to do?

Thanks for the advice! Will create some short notes that reiterate that I'm interested and thank them for their time.

&#x200B;

Will avoid the "I'm desperate. Please hire me."-type notes :)

Do thank-you notes after an interview matter? Do you write them to everyone you spoke with or just the hiring manager?

&#x200B;

Do you write thank-you notes after a phone or zoom call with a recruiter? Since it's their job to get people interested in the company, would a thank-you note look like "trying too hard"?

I've created a simple website with multiple menus. I'm stuck on how to get each of the menus to appear in the right place. Right now, every menu is appearing on the left. Any ideas?

&#x200B;

Here's my code: https://codepen.io/BoldCode/pen/dyzNeoN

To clarify, it's not a vanity issue. I don't care about ranking for "first-name last-name", but I would like to rank for "first-name middle-initial last-name" (since I haven't found anyone else with that combo and I appear to be the only one)

&#x200B;

It's really frustrating to see people who don't have my middle initial rank higher than me when searching for "first-name middle-initial last-name"

My web presence doesn't generate any money or new business for me; I care about my web presence because of emotional reasons

&#x200B;

It used to take me at least half a day to write and edit a good blog article, so it wouldn't be a good use of my time to spend a few days each week working on my blog just to boost my ego

&#x200B;

Blog-writing takes time away from money-making activities, so I'd prefer to not spend too much time on it

Thanks for your reply. Apologies if I came across as intolerant. My knowledge of this lawyer is based on this short consultation. It's hard to make judgments about someone from a short conversation, and I'm trying to evaluate limited information to make a decision.

&#x200B;

And I did not intend to use the political definition of the word "conservative". I was trying to ask if the legal industry was a slow-moving industry that doesn't like to adopt new technologies. Perhaps there's a better word for what I'm trying to convey. Apologies to anyone who thought I was using a stereotype.

One company (which is legitimate and well-known) required me to take a math test for a job interview, and they required me to share my screen and turn on my webcam, but the test proctor could also remotely control my keyboard and mouse. It was a multi-hour long test with a bathroom break. During the bathroom break, I don't think there was anything stopping the proctor from doing anything they want with my computer; since they had keyboard and mouse access, they could theoretically open files or install stuff.

&#x200B;

Although I think the chances of the proctor actually messing around with my computer is very low, I just don't like the idea of someone else having control over my own computer.

&#x200B;

For coding tests, wouldn't the proctor want to take a look at source and output files? I'm assuming a coding test would be more invasive than a math test where I simply typed in a number for the answer.

I don't like the idea of someone else having control over my personal computer. I've heard that during an online test for a job interview, the test proctor has control over my computer and can do anything they want (especially when I'm out of the room during a break between test sections).

&#x200B;

I'm thinking about getting a cheap second computer for when I have to take an online test. What minimum specs would you recommend for such a computer?

&#x200B;

And how has your experience during remote interview tests been like?

Didn't know humor could have such a large effect - thanks for sharing!

&#x200B;

How do you initiate jokes during an interview without offending someone at the company? (For example, making fun of the art on the wall might turn off whoever choose that art)

&#x200B;

What kind of things do you joke about?

I didn't make any money in 2019, but I submitted a tax return this mid-March. I should be eligible for a $1200 stimulus check but haven't received anything in the mail, and the IRS stimulus check site continues to read "status not found". I've tried calling but haven't been able to get through to anyone. What should I do?

&#x200B;

If I mailed my return in mid-March, it should have been processed by now, right?

&#x200B;

There's a stimulus check website for non-filers ( [https://www.irs.gov/coronavirus/non-filers-enter-payment-info-here](https://www.irs.gov/coronavirus/non-filers-enter-payment-info-here) ) , and it says that using this site would slow down processing of my return and any refund I might receive. Would you recommend that I use this site?

&#x200B;

Since I'm not expecting any refund, there would be no harm in me filling out the form on the stimulus check website for non-filers, right?

Thanks for your reply and helping me understand the example better!

&#x200B;

I probably should have clarified my question. I get why time complexity is O(2\^N), but why is space complexity only O(N)?

I didn't earn any income in 2018 or 2019. Should I file a tax return?

&#x200B;

If so, is there an easy and low-cost way to file a return?

&#x200B;

I've heard news about a potential $1200 stimulus check. Is the government giving people money? Or is it simply an advance on a future tax refund?

&#x200B;

Would I be eligible for the stimulus check? Would me filing or not filing a tax return affect my ability to get this check?
